with no offset, final computed position is [-1.85, 2.26, -74.04]
notable that both the bias and the noise were different on this run, but whether the cause is the hardware or the lac algorithm is unknown.
thus I will proceed to using acc, as I really should have started with - why bother with lac when you have to strip an offset out anyway?

stats on acc:
    [148 nibawr@amaranth py]$ ./scratch ../../pulled/rec_20260707_152445.csv 
    mean: [-0.014922616357225874, -0.037233321651443725, 9.771009613718084]
    sigma: [0.011409482717325085, 0.014792566921100796, 0.025079818738741485]
so the lac algorithm cut the noise by 20%:
    > 8746/11409
    [1] 0.7665878
    > 11957/14792
    [1] 0.8083423
    > 21414/25080
    [1] 0.8538278
from the central limit theorem, those ratios should be 1/sqrt(6) = 0.408...
averaging acc into buckets of 6 gives:
    [148 nibawr@amaranth py]$ ./scratch ../../pulled/rec_20260707_152445.csv 
    mean: [-0.014923212832872087, -0.03722990975277112, 9.770995872771845]
    sigma: [0.006968045864276048, 0.011460390688405021, 0.018642095831701205]

    > 6968/11409
    [1] 0.6107459
    > 11460/14792
    [1] 0.7747431
    > 18642/25080
    [1] 0.7433014
which is better than lac but not great
averaging acc into buckets of 45 (i.e. cutting to 10Hz) gives:
    [148 nibawr@amaranth py]$ ./scratch ../../pulled/rec_20260707_152445.csv 
    mean: [-0.014921524267062551, -0.03723835855228787, 9.771013175637828]
    sigma: [0.002747746098115346, 0.006101075969193909, 0.004513173446376685]
CLT expectation would be 1/sqrt(45) = 0.149...:
    > 2747/11409
    [1] 0.2407748
    > 6101/14792
    [1] 0.4124527
    > 4513/25080
    [1] 0.1799442
averaging acc into buckets of 45 also gives:
    [148 nibawr@amaranth py]$ ./scratch ../../pulled/rec_20260707_152445.csv 
    max acc: 9.794434611111111
    max diff acc: 0.07347294900222223
so this also (as expected) makes the data smoother.
looking at my 20260706-202528 ride, acceleration during the sprint phase was around 80-160 mm/s^2, and during the more regular cruise phase it ranged from occasionally
as low as 30 mm/s^2 to more typically ~120 mm/s^2 to at one point as high as 500 mm/s^2
so I think this is looking promising. the noise in the perturbation to the existing trajectory (1/2)a(dt)^2 will be (1/2)(6 mm/s^2)(0.1s)^2 = 0.03 mm over a period
that plausibly covers 50-100 cm. the noise in the perturbation to the velocity a(dt) will be (6 mm/s^2)(0.1 s) = 0.6 mm/s which over 5 seconds = 50 periods compounds to
at maximum 30 mm/s, which would create ~75 mm of error in the final position - over a period that plausibly covers 25-50 m. but this seems to be noise i.e. averaging
out to zero (it ran for 61 s without the reading seeming to drift), so really it looks like the performance will be even sigificantly better. this is almost suspiciously
good, though; we'll have to see what happens when it's under real and/or variable acceleration.

