    with magnitude: 9.810072817376179

for the rot sensor: using the values from 11% (reported by vim) of the way through the file gives:
    > theta = 2 * acos(0.9821362)
    > x = -0.002039285 / sin(theta / 2)
    > y = 9.736165e-4 / sin(theta / 2)
    > z = -0.18815824 / sin(theta / 2)
    > theta
    [1] 0.3786
    > x
    [1] -0.01083738
    > y
    [1] 0.005174093
    > z
    [1] -0.9999299
    > theta * 180 / pi
    [1] 21.69218
indicating that the phone was pointing ~22 degrees east of magnetic north. I recall the phone being pointed due north.
placing the phone in approximately the same position, the compass app i have from f-droid initially read about 20 degrees
east of magnetic north and then slowly drifted closer to north, reaching about 10 degrees after a few minutes, i think; closing and reopening
has sometimes made it start at 15 deg and sometimes reset it to 21 deg. passing it over the keyboard deck of the laptop (from the right/east side
to the left/west) deflects it to 353 deg, and moving it back puts it back to ~9-13 deg. closing and reopening with it sitting on the left causes it
to open to ~265 deg; trying it again and it's now at 25-30 deg. the compass app says to move the device in a figure-8 pattern to improve accuracy, which
worked for me once. opening the app in the middle of the street (i.e. far away from sources of magnetic fields) still started at 10 or 15 deg; figure 8
got it close to zero eventually.

so the prospects for reliably converting from the device coordinates to world coordinates are not looking good.

